# merge_player_includes() reads /etc/audiocontrol/players.d/*.json once at
# startup (main.rs), so a package dropping a player definition there - e.g.
# hifiberry-usbaudio - stays invisible until audiocontrol is restarted.
# noawait: the activating package need not wait for us, and dpkg coalesces all
# activations in a run into a single restart at the end.
interest-noawait /etc/audiocontrol/players.d

# nginx serves the client-visible API prefixes from a snippet in this
# directory, and it reads that directory only when it starts or is reloaded.
# Since 0.23.0 the metadata prefixes in our snippet point at a second daemon on
# a different port, so a config nginx loaded before the upgrade is no longer
# merely stale - it routes live requests to a daemon that no longer serves
# them, and they answer 404. Nothing warns: dpkg installs the new file, nginx
# keeps serving what it loaded at its last start, and on a device that has been
# up for weeks that is a configuration weeks old.
#
# **This interest does not fix that case, and cannot.** dpkg does not activate
# a package's own file triggers for files that package itself installs. A test
# package carrying exactly this interest and shipping a file into this
# directory was installed fresh and then upgraded twice with the file's content
# changing each time: `postinst triggered` was never called, and its status
# never left "install ok installed". A neighbouring package dropping a snippet
# here, or removing one, activated it every time. So the reload for our own
# snippet is called from postinst directly - see reload_nginx() and the call
# below #DEBHELPER# there - and this interest covers the case that call cannot
# reach.
#
# Why carry it at all, then:
#
#  - The directory is shared and nothing else reloads for it. Every package
#    dropping a snippet here has the same bug and none of them carries this
#    interest. Taking responsibility for the directory rather than only for our
#    own file fixes it once for all of them, and the daemons behind those
#    prefixes are ours to serve.
#  - It fills the hole in the other direction too. Where a neighbour changes a
#    snippet in a run that also upgrades us, dpkg drops that activation - the
#    same rule as above, measured the same way - and the configure-arm reload
#    is what covers it. Neither mechanism alone covers both, and because the
#    cases are disjoint they never reload twice for one dpkg run.
#  - Coalescing. noawait lets dpkg fold every activation in a run into a
#    single call, so an image build installing several snippet-dropping
#    packages reloads once, not once per package.
#
# The price is that a broken snippet belonging to another package is reported
# by *our* postinst. That is why reload_nginx() runs `nginx -t` first and words
# its warning as being about the configuration rather than about this package.
#
# Deliberately not covered: removing this package activates the trigger for
# anyone else interested, but our own interest goes away with the package and
# postinst is no longer there to answer it, so nginx keeps proxying prefixes
# whose daemon has gone. A dead prefix answering 502 is a far smaller problem
# than a live one answering 404, and fixing it would mean a reload from postrm
# with no snippet of ours left to validate against. Left alone.
interest-noawait /etc/nginx/hifiberry-api.d
